  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nCooler & Mostly Dry Through Midweek:\n\nAn area of cyclonic flow vertically-stacked over the Missouri River \nValley in surface observations to upper level water vapor imagery \ncontinues progressing east between two areas of upper level \nanticyclonic flow through midweek. Initial low chances for \nprecipitation advect from east to west late tonight through \nWednesday along the resultant back door cold front as the low \neventually exits east into the Ohio River Valley. Main impact will \nbe cooler, below normal temperatures with daytime highs from the 60s \nwith overnight lows in the 40s for much of the area. \n\nMidweek Precipitation Chances Mainly West:\n\nExit behavior of the upper level cyclonic flow and subsequent \nreunification of higher heights into a temporarily amplified upper \nlevel ridge from the Upper Mississippi River Valley towards the \nGreat Lakes lowers confidence in strength of incoming positive mid \nlevel vorticity advection Wednesday night into Thursday \nmorning. Current confidence in long term global ensembles keeps \nprobabilities west of the forecast area, rapidly decreasing as \nthe wave approaches the local area and interacts with the area \nof higher heights. Regardless, overall impacts appear minimal. \n\nWeekend & Early Next Week Precipitation Chances:\n\nConfidence in the synoptic area of higher mid-upper level heights \nand accompanying anticyclonic flow eventually weakening enough for \nwidespread precipitation through the weekend into early next week. \nCurrent confidence keeps positive mid level vorticity advection\nwell to the north in central Canada until late in the forecast \nperiod. Weekend precipitation chances remain low as trough and \naccompanying vorticity again weaken as they approach the Upper \nMississippi River Valley.",
